# Shared project memory

## What lives here

The engine stores written project records for your company: current status,
meeting notes, research and original attachments. Every colleague with the same
company memory can access them from their own operator workspace. Entity blobs
remain a separate synthesized memory; saving a document does not run an LLM or
extract facts into blobs.

Software documentation stays in `docs/`. Project records are accessed through
`cs project`; local checkouts are working copies and require an explicit save.
Run commands from the operator workspace root.

## Find and read

```bash
cs project list
cs project files <name>
cs project show <name>               # current status.md
cs project show <name> README.md
cs project history <name> status.md
```

Names are exact lowercase slugs. List first when unsure; do not guess a similar
customer. Read current status first, then only documents relevant to the task.
Listing does not load document bodies. For binary attachments use `show` with
its output-file option instead of interpreting bytes as text; `--help` names it.

## Start or update a project

```bash
cs project new <name> --title "Project title"
cs project checkout <name> .project-work/<name>
```

The new project has README, status, timeline and a meetings directory. Edit the
working copy using normal file tools. `.project-work/` is ignored by Git. Its
`.cs-project.json` records company identity and base revisions; do not edit it.

```bash
cs project save .project-work/<name>             # preview
cs project save .project-work/<name> --commit    # save verified changes
```

Saving creates recoverable revisions with author and timestamp. A colleague's
newer edit causes a conflict, not an overwrite. Keep your edited copy; check out
the latest project into another directory, reconcile intentionally, and save it.
Deleted local files are not deleted remotely. If a save is interrupted, retry:
unchanged successful writes are recognized rather than duplicated. A save reports
partial progress explicitly if a later document cannot be written.

A working copy belongs to one company. If the account joins another company
memory, create a new checkout there; never change the old copy's identity file.

## Import an existing project folder

```bash
cs project import docs/projects/<name>          # preview one
cs project import docs/projects/<name> --commit
cs project import docs/projects --all           # preview all project folders
cs project import docs/projects --all --commit
```

Import preserves original bytes, including binary attachments. It verifies stored
hashes and leaves the source tree in place. Equal existing files are accepted on
retry; differing files require reconciliation. Source folders retained after a
successful import are recovery copies, not the live project memory. No automatic
fallback reads them when the engine is unavailable.

Generated caches are reported as excluded; unsafe paths, symlinks, sensitive env
files and files larger than 4 MiB are refused explicitly. Each checkout/import/save
is bounded to 10,000 files and 128 MiB; larger operations require smaller
project selections. Resolve a refusal before
retrying; an incomplete import is not a completed migration. Top-level legacy
README and meeting templates are conventions, not company project folders.

## Record shape and evidence

| Document | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| `README.md` | Project identity, abstract, contacts and document index |
| `status.md` | Agreed scope, current obligations, deadlines, risks and next actions |
| `timeline.md` | Dated chronology, sources and links to meeting notes |
| `meetings/*` | Meeting records; append dated corrections instead of rewriting history |
| Other documents | Research, proposals, dossiers and original attachments |

Keep status in one place. Write short abstracts so the next operator can choose
what to read. Attribute claims with `[mail]`, `[meeting]`, `[confirmed]`,
`[reported]`, `[inferred]` or `[to verify]`; retain dates and provenance. Use
English for authored notes, preserving the original language of direct quotes and
customer copy. No passwords, tokens or credentials belong in project memory.

Start meeting notes from [the meeting template](project-meeting-template.md),
in the selected working copy's `meetings/` folder. Add the event to timeline.md
and save explicitly. Revision storage preserves bytes; the append-only meeting
convention is an authoring responsibility, not a server content validator.

Use the customer skill before customer work. Reconcile authored documents with
entity memory and current correspondence by evidence and dates. No storage layer
wins a factual conflict merely by being stored in the engine. Facts worth adding
to entity memory use the separate explicit memory-write procedure in that skill.
Shared project writes themselves never create drafts, send messages or train AI.
